1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,040
Let’s look at one line of code together.

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:08,530
Early Swift let you declare a variable inside the parentheses after a function name.

3
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:13,460
If we change the number inside, what value will we see outside?

4
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:17,080
First, we pass the number 1 into the function.

5
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:19,530
The number inside becomes 2.

6
00:00:19,980 --> 00:00:22,780
The variable outside still holds 1.

7
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:26,190
Inside the function, we changed a copy of the integer.

8
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:32,290
This time we put an ampersand before the variable name in the call.

9
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:34,660
The number inside becomes 2.

10
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:39,030
When the function finishes, 2 is written back to the variable outside.

11
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:45,000
Both allowed changes inside the function, so it was easy to miss the difference.

12
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,860
A proposal to reduce this confusion was recorded in November 2015.

13
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:55,710
Swift 2.2, released the following March, issued a warning.

14
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:00,080
With Swift 3 that September, this declaration became an error.

15
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:04,500
The original proposal covered syntax beyond function parameters.

16
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:08,950
For example, we can unwrap a value, modify it, and return it.

17
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:14,920
Under the original proposal, we would have to declare another variable after unwrapping the value.

18
00:01:15,370 --> 00:01:19,610
After examining real code, the author asked to withdraw the entire change.

19
00:01:20,910 --> 00:01:23,710
The core team made separate decisions for the two cases.

20
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:30,720
They found that exposing a function’s local variable in its signature could mislead callers.

21
00:01:31,170 --> 00:01:36,690
In a conditional, binding a value for immediate editing was useful, so that syntax stayed.

22
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:44,070
Today, if we want to change a copy, we declare a local variable inside the function.

23
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:47,720
In our opening example, the number outside stays at 1.

24
00:01:48,170 --> 00:01:53,370
We can still use a local variable. What changed is where we declare it.

25
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:55,100
We’ll stop here for today.
